From fa469c015838d9cd9adde3368a919f1512f5c867 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AOSP RK3576 Baseline <uhf-platform@localbuild>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 16:03:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] uhf: fix the CRC16 polynomial - confirmed wrong on real
 hardware

Real blocker, reported from actual reader hardware, not an inference
this time: every command timed out, UART tx counters advancing while rx
stayed at zero since boot - the reader never once replied to the HAL,
though it answered a shell probe on the same port in ~100ms.

Proven back to back on the wire with the HAL stopped:

  04 ff 21 b1 e9  -- CRC-16/MODBUS (poly 0xA001), our previous guess -> silent
  04 ff 21 19 95  -- poly 0x8408, same init/algorithm otherwise       -> full reply

Independently reproduced here: computing both polynomials over the same
three leading bytes (04 ff 21) with this class's own init (0xFFFF) and
bit-reflected structure gives exactly b1 e9 for 0xA001 and exactly 19 95
for 0x8408 - and the full 5-byte captured frame (04 ff 21 19 95) CRCs to
zero under 0x8408, confirming both the polynomial and that nothing else
in the frame/CRC structure needs to change.

This was always isolated to exactly one constant on purpose (see the
class's own prior doc comment) specifically so a wrong guess would be
cheap to fix - changed CRC16's poly literal from 0xA001 to 0x8408 and
rewrote the class/method doc to state the confirmed algorithm instead of
the MODBUS inference. Added a new test,
crc16_matchesRealHardwareCapture, pinning the actual captured bytes -
every other CRC test in this class is a round-trip property (build then
validate, or determinism) that holds for any polynomial including the
wrong one, which is exactly why none of them caught this; a real
hardware-derived vector is the only kind of test that can.

Verified by actually compiling and running the full UhfProtocolTest
suite (all 38 tests, including the new one) with real javac + JUnit 4,
not just by inspection.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocol.java   | 33 ++++++++++---------
 .../android/server/uhf/UhfProtocolTest.java   | 16 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 33 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocol.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocol.java
index e10aa9f7..d82851e3 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocol.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocol.java
@@ -31,17 +31,17 @@ import java.util.List;
  * with the highest cost of being wrong (FR-LNK-06's note describes a corrupted-command failure
  * mode that a naive implementation reproduces easily).
  *
- * <p><b>What is settled vs. assumed.</b> The frame shape, the requirement that CRC over the whole
- * frame (including its own CRC bytes) come out to zero, the resync rule, and the reCmd-matching
- * rule are all directly and unambiguously stated in UHF-SRS-001 &sect;4.3 and are implemented
- * exactly as specified. The one thing the SRS does not pin down is the CRC16 variant: "CRC16" is
- * an algorithm family, not one algorithm. This class assumes CRC-16/MODBUS (reflected, poly
- * 0xA001, init 0xFFFF, no xorout) because that is the variant used by every other reader in this
- * family's lineage that exposes this exact frame shape, but until R1/R3 is on disk (SRS OI-01)
- * this is an inference, not a verified fact — see {@link #CRC16_INIT}. If it is wrong, exactly one
- * constant changes; every test in UhfProtocolTest that depends on it is written against that same
- * constant, not against a hand-computed magic number, so a wrong assumption fails loudly instead
- * of silently.
+ * <p><b>CRC16 variant — confirmed on real hardware, not an inference.</b> The frame shape, the
+ * requirement that CRC over the whole frame (including its own CRC bytes) come out to zero, the
+ * resync rule, and the reCmd-matching rule are all directly and unambiguously stated in
+ * UHF-SRS-001 &sect;4.3 and are implemented exactly as specified. The CRC16 variant was originally
+ * an inference (CRC-16/MODBUS, poly 0xA001) because that is the variant used by every other reader
+ * in this family's lineage that exposes this exact frame shape — but that inference was wrong.
+ * Proven back to back on the wire with a real reader, HAL stopped: the frame {@code 04 ff 21} with
+ * the MODBUS CRC ({@code b1 e9}) was silently ignored; the identical frame with poly 0x8408
+ * instead ({@code 19 95}) got a full reply. Same init (0xFFFF), same reflected/LSB-first
+ * structure — only the polynomial constant differs, exactly as the "isolated to one constant"
+ * design here was meant to allow. See {@link #CRC16_INIT}.
  */
 public final class UhfProtocol {
 
@@ -54,8 +54,8 @@ public final class UhfProtocol {
     public static final int MAX_LEN_FIELD = 0xFF;
 
     /**
-     * CRC-16/MODBUS initial register value. See the class doc: this is an inference pending R1,
-     * isolated to this one constant on purpose.
+     * CRC16 initial register value — confirmed correct on real hardware alongside the polynomial
+     * below. See the class doc for the byte-level proof.
      */
     static final int CRC16_INIT = 0xFFFF;
 
@@ -64,8 +64,9 @@ public final class UhfProtocol {
     // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 
     /**
-     * Computes CRC-16/MODBUS (reflected, poly 0xA001, init {@link #CRC16_INIT}, xorout 0) over
-     * {@code length} bytes of {@code data} starting at {@code offset}.
+     * Computes this reader's CRC16 (reflected, poly 0x8408, init {@link #CRC16_INIT}, xorout 0)
+     * over {@code length} bytes of {@code data} starting at {@code offset}. See the class doc for
+     * the real-hardware proof of the polynomial.
      */
     public static int crc16(byte[] data, int offset, int length) {
         if (data == null) {
@@ -82,7 +83,7 @@ public final class UhfProtocol {
                 boolean lsbSet = (crc & 0x0001) != 0;
                 crc >>>= 1;
                 if (lsbSet) {
-                    crc ^= 0xA001;
+                    crc ^= 0x8408;
                 }
             }
         }
diff --git a/services/tests/servicestests/src/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocolTest.java b/services/tests/servicestests/src/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocolTest.java
index 1fa61951..fda105a3 100644
--- a/services/tests/servicestests/src/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocolTest.java
+++ b/services/tests/servicestests/src/com/android/server/uhf/UhfProtocolTest.java
@@ -82,6 +82,22 @@ public class UhfProtocolTest {
         }
     }
 
+    /**
+     * The one test in this class that isn't self-consistent by construction: every other CRC test
+     * here checks a round-trip property (build then validate, or determinism) that holds for
+     * <em>any</em> polynomial, including the wrong one this class shipped with for a while
+     * (CRC-16/MODBUS, poly 0xA001) — which is exactly why none of them caught it. This pins the
+     * actual bytes captured off a real reader, HAL stopped, address 0xFF (broadcast) / command
+     * 0x21 (identify), no payload: {@code 04 ff 21 19 95}. With the wrong polynomial the same
+     * three leading bytes produce {@code b1 e9}, a frame the reader silently ignores.
+     */
+    @Test
+    public void crc16_matchesRealHardwareCapture() {
+        byte[] frame = {0x04, (byte) 0xFF, 0x21, 0x19, (byte) 0x95};
+        assertEquals(0, UhfProtocol.crc16(frame, 0, frame.length));
+        assertTrue(UhfProtocol.isCrcValid(frame, 0, frame.length));
+    }
+
     @Test
     public void crc16_flippingAnyByteInvalidatesTheFrame() {
         byte[] frame = UhfProtocol.buildFrame(0xFF, 0x21, new byte[] {1, 2, 3, 4, 5});
